==Professional career==
===2012 X-League Draft===
Kongo was selected 3rd overall in the first round of the 2012 X-League Draft by the [[Okami Direwolves]]. The selection was considered a coup: the Direwolves, who already featured defensive end [[Kengo Shibata|Kengo "The Wall" Shibata]], added the most talented linebacker prospect in a generation to form what would become the most feared defensive duo in the league.

[[File:2012 X-League Draft Naoto Kongo.png|thumb|left|Kongo at the 2012 X-League Draft after being selected 3rd overall by the Okami Direwolves]]

===2012: Rookie season===
Kongo started all 14 games at middle linebacker as a rookie. Defensive coordinator Hisashi Morimoto installed Kongo as the "Mike" linebacker—the quarterback of the defense—and gave him full play-calling authority from the first week. Kongo responded with 118 total tackles, 4.5 sacks, and 2 interceptions, earning X-League Rookie of the Year honors and his first All-Star selection. His tackle total led all rookies and ranked third in Division 1.

===2013: Breakout and first Defensive Player of the Year===
In his second season, Kongo elevated from promising rookie to the league's preeminent defensive force. He posted 132 tackles, 6 sacks, and 2 interceptions, including a dominant Week 8 performance against the [[Toyozawa Ironblades]] where he recorded 14 tackles, 2 sacks, and a forced fumble. He was named Division 1 Defensive Player of the Year for the first time and earned his second consecutive All-Star selection.

The Kongo–Shibata axis defined the Direwolves' defense. With Shibata commanding double-teams on the edge, Kongo operated with unusual freedom in the middle of the field, diagnosing plays with pre-snap reads that bordered on clairvoyant. The Direwolves' defense allowed a league-low 187 points in 2013.

===2014: Continued dominance===
Kongo's third season maintained his elite standard: 126 tackles, 5.5 sacks, and 1 interception across 14 games. While the Direwolves fell short in the Division 1 playoff semifinal against the [[Soma Samurai|Saitama Forges]], Kongo's individual performance earned him a third consecutive All-Star selection. He also recorded his first career defensive touchdown on a 42-yard interception return against the [[Sendai Blitz]] in Week 5.

===2015: Rice Bowl championship===
The 2015 season was the pinnacle of Kongo's Lurandian Football career. He posted 128 tackles, 5 sacks, and 2 interceptions during the regular season, but it was his postseason performance that cemented his legacy.

[[File:2016 Rice Bowl Naoto Kongo.png|thumb|Kongo during the 2016 Rice Bowl, where he was named MVP]]

In the [[2016 Rice Bowl]] (played January 10, 2016), the Direwolves faced the heavily favored [[Toyozawa Ironblades]], who had posted the league's best regular-season record behind the rushing attack of [[Ryuichi Nakada]]. Kongo's game plan—calling every defensive signal from the middle—was to shadow Nakada with a spy linebacker while rotating coverage to take away the play-action pass. The result: Nakada was held to 47 rushing yards, his lowest output of the season, and the Direwolves won 17–14.

Kongo recorded 11 tackles, 1 sack, and a critical fourth-quarter forced fumble that killed the Ironblades' final drive. He was named the [[Rice Bowl MVP]], becoming the first middle linebacker in X-League history to receive the honor.

===2016–2017: Peak years===
Kongo's 2016 and 2017 seasons represented the statistical peak of his career. In 2016, despite missing two games with a shoulder injury, he recorded 98 tackles and a career-high 7 sacks in 12 games, earning his second Division 1 Defensive Player of the Year award. His Week 4 performance—17 tackles, 3 sacks, and 1 forced fumble against the [[Shinagawa Kraken|Fukuoka Kraken]]—is widely considered the greatest single-game performance by a linebacker in X-League history.

In 2017, fully healthy, Kongo posted 110 tackles, 5 sacks, and 1 interception across 14 games, earning his third Defensive Player of the Year award and sixth consecutive All-Star selection. He and Shibata anchored a Direwolves defense that surrendered just 168 points all season, the fewest in Division 1.

===2018: Injuries and decline===
The 2018 season marked the beginning of the end. Kongo suffered a torn left labrum in Week 3 and missed four games—the first significant injury of his career. He returned in Week 8 but was visibly limited, unable to shed blocks with his customary violence. He finished with 88 tackles, 3.5 sacks, and 1 interception in 12 games, respectable numbers by league standards but a clear decline from his peak.

The shoulder injury lingered through the offseason. Kongo rehabbed intensively—an approach that would later prove foundational to his bodybuilding career—but the cumulative toll of six seasons of interior linebacker play was becoming impossible to ignore.

===2019: Final season===
Kongo's 2019 season was a farewell tour in plain sight. He started 12 games, recording 76 tackles, 2 sacks, and 0 interceptions—his lowest statistical output since his rookie year. While his football IQ and leadership remained elite, his body could no longer sustain the relentless physicality that had defined his game.

Privately, Kongo had already begun training for competitive bodybuilding during the offseason, finding that the iron provided something the gridiron no longer could: a relationship with the forge that was personal rather than strategic.

===Retirement===
On January 18, 2020, three days after the Rice Bowl, Kongo announced his retirement from the X-League at age 28. In a press conference at the Okami Direwolves' facility, he cited the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} philosophy as the foundation of his decision:

{{quote|The iron on the field gave me everything—brotherhood, purpose, the forge. But the iron in the gym is calling me in a different way now. I've spent my whole life hitting people. Now I want to spend the next half of my life building something. The body is the forge. The iron is the will. I'm not walking away from the Cult of Iron. I'm deepening it.}}

[[File:Naoto Kongo retirement press conference 2020.png|thumb|left|Kongo at his retirement press conference, January 2020]]

The announcement sent shockwaves through the X-League. Kongo was the league's most recognizable defensive player, and his retirement at 28—while still productive—drew inevitable comparisons to early retirements in other gridiron codes. Direwolves head coach Yusuke Takeda called it "the greatest loss this franchise has ever absorbed," while Shibata released a statement calling Kongo "the best linebacker I ever lined up beside, and the best friend I ever made on a football field."

The Okami Direwolves retired Kongo's No. 36 jersey in a ceremony before the 2020 season opener, alongside longtime teammate Kengo Shibata's No. 90.

==Playing style==
Kongo was the archetypal middle linebacker for the Lurandian Football code: a punishing tackler with rare sideline-to-sideline range, exceptional diagnostic instincts, and the physical durability to absorb and deliver collision after collision in the interior of the field. At 185 cm and 108 kg (238 lbs), he possessed the thick, compact build ideal for the position—enough mass to stack blocks at the line of scrimmage, enough speed to chase down sweeps and screen passes in the Lurandian Football's wider 65-yard field.

His signature trait was pre-snap recognition. Kongo routinely identified the offensive play based on formation, personnel, and motion cues, calling audibles that shifted the Direwolves' front into the optimal alignment before the snap. Opposing quarterbacks widely regarded him as the most difficult linebacker to scheme against in the league, because his calls effectively neutralized the unlimited pre-snap motion that is the hallmark of Lurandian Football offenses.

As a pass rusher, Kongo was devastating on delayed blitzes—a scheme weapon that defensive coordinator Morimoto designed specifically to exploit Kongo's instincts. Rather than rushing on every snap, Kongo would read the quarterback's eyes from the middle of the field, then time his rush to arrive at the throw point unblocked. His 38.5 career sacks are the second-most by a middle linebacker in X-League history.

In coverage, Kongo was adequate but not elite. His 11 career interceptions came primarily from reading the quarterback's eyes and jumping underneath routes, rather than from man-coverage ability. His 34 passes defensed reflect a player who disrupted passing lanes with his physical presence rather than his agility.

==Bodybuilding career==
Kongo's transition from Lurandian Football to competitive bodybuilding was not a pivot—it was a return. He had grown up in the {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology, where physical conditioning was a matter of national identity and the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} was the national religion. Bodybuilding was not a hobby; it was a sacred practice.

[[File:Toyozawa Iron Temple exterior.png|thumb|left|The Toyozawa Iron Temple, where Kongo has trained since his youth]]

During his football career, Kongo was renowned for his offseason training intensity, which teammates described as "more punishing than the regular season." He trained at the [[Toyozawa Iron Temple]], a gym owned by former Goriki competitor [[Goro Takada|"The Anvil" Takada]], and followed a hypertrophy-focused program that maintained his playing weight while building the dense, striated muscle that would later define his bodybuilding physique.

After retiring in 2020, Kongo transitioned to a dedicated bodybuilding program under the guidance of coach Minoru Fujita, a former Iron Sakura Classic competitor. He competed in the super-heavyweight division (100+ kg) and quickly established himself as a dominant force on the Okaiken national circuit.

===Iron Sakura Classic===
Kongo made his competitive debut at the 2021 Iron Sakura Classic, placing third—a result he described as "humbling and instructive." He returned in 2022 with a dramatically improved package, featuring the deep striations and vacuum-like conditioning that the Iron Sakura Classic judges reward, and won the super-heavyweight title. He repeated as champion in 2023, becoming the first former X-League player to win the Iron Sakura Classic and the first competitor to win back-to-back titles since [[Kazuki Hino|"The Iron Monk" Hino]] in 2015–2016.

[[File:Naoto Kongo Iron Sakura Classic 2023.png|thumb|Kongo at the 2023 Iron Sakura Classic, winning his second consecutive title]]

===Okaiken National Grand Prix===
In 2024, Kongo entered the [[Okaiken National Grand Prix]], the most prestigious bodybuilding competition in Okaiken. He won the super-heavyweight class and the overall title, defeating a field that included several former Iron Sakura champions. His victory was widely celebrated in the Forge State media as a validation of the Cult of Iron's philosophy: the body, forged in competition and refined in discipline, can reach the pinnacle of multiple athletic domains.

===Training philosophy===
Kongo's bodybuilding training philosophy is deeply influenced by his Lurandian Football background. He employs block periodization—accumulation, intensification, realization, and deload—mirroring the annual cycle he used as a football player. His training splits emphasize heavy compound movements (squat, deadlift, bench press) in the accumulation phase, with isolation work increasing during intensification. He trains at the [[Toyozawa Iron Temple]], where the gym culture is rooted in the Cult of Iron's emphasis on physical discipline as spiritual practice.

===Personal training===
Kongo currently serves as a personal trainer and bodybuilding coach, working with clients who seek to integrate the Cult of Iron philosophy into their own training. His coaching style emphasizes periodization, progressive overload, and the mental discipline he developed as a linebacker—reading the body's signals the way he once read offensive formations.

==Personal life==
Kongo resides in [[Toyozawa]], [[Okaiken]], and continues to train at the [[Toyozawa Iron Temple]]. He remains close with former teammate [[Kengo Shibata]], and the two are frequently seen together at X-League games and bodybuilding events. Kongo has been a vocal advocate for the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} in popular media, appearing on OR Sports programs and in Samurai TV documentaries about the Forge State's athletic culture.

Kongo has spoken openly about the chronic pain he experiences from his football career—including the torn labrum that ended his 2018 season—and credits bodybuilding's emphasis on controlled, deliberate movement with helping him manage the physical toll of interior linebacker play. He has advocated for improved injury protocols in the X-League, particularly for defensive players who absorb repeated high-impact collisions.

==Legacy==
Kongo is widely regarded as the greatest middle linebacker in X-League history. His 876 career tackles remain the Okami Direwolves' franchise record, and his 38.5 sacks are the second-most by a middle linebacker in league history. The Kongo–Shibata {{nihongo|"Iron Curtain"|鉄のカーテン|Tetsu no Kāten}} defense of 2013–2017 is considered one of the most dominant defensive units in Lurandian Football history, and the 2015 Rice Bowl victory—where Kongo's game plan neutralized the league's best rushing attack—remains the standard for middle linebacker performance in championship games.

His transition to bodybuilding made him a cultural icon in the Forge State, embodying the Cult of Iron's promise that the body can be forged across disciplines. He is the only athlete to have won both a Rice Bowl MVP and an Iron Sakura Classic title, a distinction that ''Toyozawa Shimbun'' sports columnist Kenji Yamada called "the ultimate expression of the Cult of Iron—a man who dominated with the iron in his hands, then dominated again with the iron on his back."

